{"sec": "A", "topic": "analogies", "diff": 2, "q": "H : L :: D : ?", "opts": ["Z", "I", "H", "G"], "ans": 2, "exp": "Principle: letter analogies usually encode a fixed forward or backward jump along A-to-Z — treat each letter as its position number (A=1...Z=26), measure the jump once, and repeat it identically. Step 1: find how far H moved to become L in the alphabet — count forward: it's +4 positions. Step 2: verify there's no shorter/alternate explanation (e.g. reverse-order or vowel-consonant swap) — a simple shift fits perfectly. Step 3: apply the same +4 shift to D: D + 4 positions = H. The distractors use a wrong shift amount or the wrong direction (−4 instead of +4). Exam tip: watch for wraparound near Z→A — count positions on your fingers or write the alphabet strip if the shift crosses that boundary.", "tags": ["analogies"], "id": "R598"},
{"sec": "A", "topic": "coding", "diff": 3, "q": "In a code, odd-position letters move +2 and even-position letters move −2 (positions start at 1). LAW becomes:", "opts": ["NCY", "JYU", "WAL", "NYY"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Principle: some codes apply a DIFFERENT rule depending on where a letter sits in the word — always read the position rule first (odd vs even) before touching any letter, since applying one uniform shift to the whole word is the classic trap. Step 1: number each letter's position in LAW starting at 1, and note whether it's odd or even. Step 2: shift odd-position letters forward by 2 and even-position letters backward by 2 — never apply the same shift to all letters (that's the trap in the distractors). Step 3: letter by letter: L(pos1)→N A(pos2)→Y W(pos3)→Y, giving NYY. Exam tip: mark each letter with a small 'o' or 'e' above it for odd/even position before shifting — this prevents mixing up which direction applies where.", "tags": ["coding"], "id": "R599"},
{"sec": "A", "topic": "series", "diff": 1, "q": "3, 6, 9, 12, 15, ?", "opts": ["19", "15", "21", "18"], "ans": 3, "exp": "Principle: the first thing to try on any number series is the plain difference between consecutive terms — if that difference is constant, the series is arithmetic and the next term just continues adding it. Step 1: write the difference between each pair of consecutive terms: 3, 3, 3, 3. Step 2: since all differences equal 3, this is a simple arithmetic series. Step 3: extend the pattern by adding 3 to the last term: 15 + 3 = 18. Exam tip: don't stop after checking just one gap — confirm the SAME difference repeats across all consecutive pairs before trusting it, since a single matching gap can be a coincidence.", "tags": ["series"], "id": "R600"}
